      "claim": "TSU-68 had 42% absolute oral bioavailability after meals in humans, supporting F ≥ 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C17"],
      "claim": "Nevirapine had 91 ± 8% absolute oral bioavailability in healthy volunteers, supporting high oral bioavailability.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C52"],
      "claim": "Ofloxacin had 95–100% absolute oral bioavailability in volunteers, supporting high oral bioavailability.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Sunitinib had approximately 50% absolute oral bioavailability in fasted humans, supporting F ≥ 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C48"],
      "claim": "Amphotericin B had negligible oral bioavailability, but this evidence comes from a structurally distinct polyene macrolide with extreme polarity and is outweighed by the rifamycin/ansamycin context of the query.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is rifampicin, a rifamycin ansamycin with a naphthohydroquinone core and N-methylpiperazine hydrazone. Multiple high-transfer experimental analogues show absolute oral bioavailability well above 20% (27–100%), including BI 425809, trametinib, TSU-68, nevirapine, ofloxacin, and sunitinib. The only low-F evidence (amphotericin B) is from a structurally distant polyene macrolide and is outweighed. Therefore predict high (F ≥ 20%).",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule itself in the provided evidence; prediction relies on transferable analogues.",
    "The amphotericin B low-F evidence is structurally distant and may not reflect rifamycin absorption; direct rifampicin data would remove residual uncertainty."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

